# REQUIRES: c166-registered-target
# RUN: rm -rf %t && mkdir -p %t
# RUN: llvm-mc -filetype=obj -triple=c166 %S/Inputs/gdb-stub.s -o %t/prog.o
# RUN: ld.lld -Ttext=0xc00100 -e _start %t/prog.o -o %t/prog.elf

## The register numbers the GDB remote protocol uses, which are the stub's own
## and are not the DWARF ones.
##
## "p" and "P" take the number from the target description where the client
## read one, and otherwise the position in the "g" packet - so the description
## has to advertise those positions or the two disagree.  It used to advertise
## the DWARF numbers instead, which are what the unwind information is written
## in and are a different numbering: 0 to 15 for R0 to R15 and then 32 upward.
## A client that read the description agreed with the stub, and a client that
## did not got R0 to R15 right by coincidence and nothing else - which is
## exactly what LLDB does, having no XML parser unless it is built with one.
##
## The bug survived because the only client in this tree reads whole register
## dumps with "g" and never asks for one register, so nothing exercised "p" at
## all.  These do.

# RUN: %c166_rsp -- %c166_sim --gdb %t/prog.elf < %s.commands 2>&1 \
# RUN:   | FileCheck %s

## R0 is register 0 either way, which is why the old numbering looked right.
# CHECK: p0 -> 0000

## PSW is the seventeenth register in the "g" packet, so it is 10H.  Under the
## old numbering this was the DWARF number 32 and 10H found nothing.
# CHECK: p10 -> 0000

## And the last one, the program counter: number 1DH, four bytes rather than
## two, holding CSP:IP.  The program is linked at C0'0100H, and the protocol
## carries a register least significant byte first - so 00'C0'01'00 comes back
## as the bytes 00 01 C0 00.
# CHECK: p1d -> 0001c000

## Writing goes by the same number, in the same byte order: this sets R2 to
## 1234H and reads back the same four digits.
# CHECK: P2=3412 -> OK
# CHECK: p2 -> 3412

## A number past the end is an error rather than a read of whatever is there.
# CHECK: p1e -> E01

## The description says both numberings, each in the attribute meant for it.
# CHECK: regnum="16" dwarf_regnum="32"
# CHECK: regnum="29" dwarf_regnum="45"
